00:04:49.210 --> 00:04:53.560
- Impressive. You'll have to let
us know how it goes. Well, Ben,

00:04:53.560 --> 00:04:56.110
something I thought might be
interesting to kick off the

00:04:56.110 --> 00:04:59.110
conversation with as we're
getting questions from

00:04:59.110 --> 00:04:59.995
everybody here is,

00:05:01.030 --> 00:05:05.020
I know that you were a
consultant in a previous role

00:05:05.020 --> 00:05:08.180
and might be good to hear
a little bit about your journey.

00:05:08.180 --> 00:05:13.030
But also love to hear what
was it like transitioning from data

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:16.960
science as a consultant to data
science in the pharma industry too.

00:05:17.740 --> 00:05:18.240
- Yeah.

00:05:18.760 --> 00:05:23.440
So how I became a data
scientist, it's like, it's pretty funny.

00:05:24.730 --> 00:05:25.750
I went to--

00:05:25.750 --> 00:05:30.150
at university, I studied
like quantitative economics and geospatial

00:05:31.150 --> 00:05:35.050
information systems and became a
GIS developer right out of

00:05:35.050 --> 00:05:38.170
college working for the United
States Postal Service on a

00:05:38.170 --> 00:05:42.610
federal contract. And I worked
on like route optimization. And

00:05:43.330 --> 00:05:46.090
I was like, this is pretty
cool. I really like doing this.

00:05:46.090 --> 00:05:48.860
And then the term like
big data came around,

00:05:48.860 --> 00:05:50.780
and people became
data scientists.

00:05:50.780 --> 00:05:54.900
And I thought to myself,
oh, wow. That's pretty cool.

00:05:54.900 --> 00:05:58.280
Maybe I should just do that
instead of like being a GIS developer

00:05:58.880 --> 00:06:01.740
and really get into data.

00:06:01.740 --> 00:06:03.950
So that's what I ended up
doing is deciding, all right,

00:06:03.950 --> 00:06:05.225
I'm going to focus more on--

00:06:06.980 --> 00:06:10.380
more on data. I can always
go back to GIS later.

00:06:10.380 --> 00:06:12.710
But really focus on trying to
understand how do I deal with

00:06:12.710 --> 00:06:13.910
really large data sets

00:06:14.540 --> 00:06:16.260
to solve different problems.

00:06:16.260 --> 00:06:19.220
So I ended up
becoming a consultant.

00:06:19.220 --> 00:06:20.180
I worked in

00:06:20.810 --> 00:06:23.600
commercial consulting,
specifically for cybersecurity

00:06:23.600 --> 00:06:25.565
clients, building
out different tools,

00:06:26.390 --> 00:06:29.450
platforms, implementing
machine learning methodologies,

00:06:29.450 --> 00:06:30.540
things like that.

00:06:30.540 --> 00:06:33.920
And it was a really great way
to get exposure to lots of

00:06:33.920 --> 00:06:36.245
different problems, as well as

00:06:37.160 --> 00:06:39.140
being able to
build up my skills

00:06:40.310 --> 00:06:43.250
for things like I might
not have learned at school.

00:06:44.390 --> 00:06:45.290
And eventually,

00:06:45.290 --> 00:06:48.620
what did I end up doing is
transitioning into the pharma

00:06:48.620 --> 00:06:51.110
industry through to a great

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:55.310
leadership program that GSK
has called the Esprit Program,

00:06:55.310 --> 00:06:59.510
which basically looks to
bring outside talent who has a

00:06:59.510 --> 00:07:02.510
specific knowledge base and bring
it into GSK that they

00:07:02.510 --> 00:07:06.770
want to expand upon. So
my cohort of Esprit candidates

00:07:06.770 --> 00:07:08.210
were all data scientists.

00:07:08.210 --> 00:07:13.140
And GSK biostats wanted to
bring data science expertise in-house,

00:07:13.880 --> 00:07:17.220
and they do it
through this three-year leadership program.

00:07:17.220 --> 00:07:21.950
So what we do basically
is we do rotational programs through

00:07:21.950 --> 00:07:24.260
different groups within
the biostats organization,

00:07:24.260 --> 00:07:27.020
bring our data science
expertise, and then they teach

00:07:27.020 --> 00:07:31.250
us the pharmaceutical
industry business essentially during

00:07:31.250 --> 00:07:34.160
those three years. And then at
the end of the three years,

00:07:34.160 --> 00:07:38.690
you find a
final position within biostats.

00:07:38.690 --> 00:07:41.720
But it's a great way
to think about, oh,

00:07:41.720 --> 00:07:44.400
this person has some
interesting talent that we want

00:07:44.400 --> 00:07:47.120
but they don't have the
business knowledge. How do we marry

00:07:47.900 --> 00:07:48.400
the

00:07:49.400 --> 00:07:52.560
lack of business knowledge with the
talent that we want to build?

00:07:52.560 --> 00:07:56.060
And I think that's one thing
that I really love about GSK.

00:07:56.060 --> 00:07:59.540
And sorry to be like a
GSK promoter right now but being

00:07:59.540 --> 00:08:02.210
able to have that kind of space
to be able to learn and develop

00:08:02.210 --> 00:08:06.500
is really crucial to being
able to create those good outcomes

00:08:06.500 --> 00:08:07.085
for the business.

00:08:08.900 --> 00:08:10.460
- Yeah. I'll echo
what Ben said.

00:08:10.460 --> 00:08:14.350
It sounds like a great program
that a lot of other companies

00:08:14.350 --> 00:08:15.475
may benefit from too.

00:08:16.120 --> 00:08:20.530
And also realizing there's a
ton of love for geospatial in

00:08:20.530 --> 00:08:22.540
the chat here too.

00:08:22.540 --> 00:08:23.040
- Yeah.

00:08:23.980 --> 00:08:26.410
That's cool to see about
all the people that love--

00:08:26.410 --> 00:08:28.075
that love GIS.

00:08:31.210 --> 00:08:32.995
- I'd love to hear a
little bit more about

00:08:34.130 --> 00:08:37.280
what it's like going through
those rotations too and

00:08:37.280 --> 00:08:39.470
teaching people data science.

00:08:39.470 --> 00:08:41.225
Imagine you're doing
a lot of training.

00:08:42.710 --> 00:08:45.800
And what it's like them
teaching you the pharma side of

00:08:45.800 --> 00:08:47.795
things as well. Could
you give us an example?

00:08:49.100 --> 00:08:49.600
- Yeah.

00:08:50.390 --> 00:08:51.490
So one

00:08:52.310 --> 00:08:55.960
of the things that came up
a lot of different times was really,

00:08:56.780 --> 00:08:58.220
so if you think about pharma,

00:08:58.220 --> 00:09:00.290
we're a highly
regulated industry.

00:09:01.290 --> 00:09:03.290
And I think one of
the things that--

00:09:03.290 --> 00:09:06.125
one of the conversations I
often had with people was,

00:09:07.190 --> 00:09:07.730
well,

00:09:07.730 --> 00:09:10.610
we can't do this because
we're a highly regulated industry.

00:09:10.610 --> 00:09:12.890
And to go back to your
previous question about what I learned

00:09:12.890 --> 00:09:14.060
from consulting,

00:09:14.840 --> 00:09:16.910
because of the nature
of my work previously,

00:09:16.910 --> 00:09:19.520
I worked in highly regulated
industries but we were able to

00:09:19.520 --> 00:09:21.390
use open source tools.

00:09:21.390 --> 00:09:24.605
So I think one of the
things that was really interesting going through

00:09:25.280 --> 00:09:28.130
the rotational program and
talking to different business

00:09:28.130 --> 00:09:32.480
groups and things like that was
being able to have that outside

00:09:32.480 --> 00:09:35.810
pharma industry perspective
to say like, look,

00:09:35.810 --> 00:09:40.280
you're highly regulated but
so is a lot of automobile

00:09:40.280 --> 00:09:43.145
companies or oil and gas or XYZ

00:09:43.970 --> 00:09:45.570
other companies as well.

00:09:45.570 --> 00:09:47.990
And they're able to
use open source tools.

00:09:47.990 --> 00:09:51.270
I think you might have to
think about it differently for your specific

00:09:51.950 --> 00:09:52.450
industry.

00:09:53.510 --> 00:09:57.140
But if you look at
like pure accounts of regulations,

00:09:57.140 --> 00:10:00.830
I think automotive and oil
and gas and financial services all

00:10:00.830 --> 00:10:06.600
have more laws and regulations
compared to pharma. So I think

00:10:06.600 --> 00:10:09.730
one of the things that was
interesting was going through those rotations

00:10:10.710 --> 00:10:12.500
and consistently having that

00:10:13.200 --> 00:10:14.600
conversation that you

00:10:15.980 --> 00:10:16.480
don't

00:10:17.000 --> 00:10:18.710
just default because
you're highly regulated,

00:10:18.710 --> 00:10:19.760
you can't use these tools,

00:10:19.760 --> 00:10:23.450
it's figuring out how do
you use them safely within

00:10:24.290 --> 00:10:27.920
the organization to make
sure you're satisfying those requirements.

00:10:28.670 --> 00:10:31.010
- I got excited
by you describing that program.

00:10:31.010 --> 00:10:34.040
And I jumped way ahead to
what you learned as a consultant.

00:10:34.040 --> 00:10:37.415
But are there other things you
wanted to expand on about that?

00:10:39.830 --> 00:10:42.540
- What else I learned
about consultant?

00:10:43.040 --> 00:10:44.390
How to tell a good story.

00:10:45.380 --> 00:10:48.290
I think that's one thing
that constantly when I'm working

00:10:48.290 --> 00:10:50.990
with data scientists who
might be newer is like, how

00:10:51.950 --> 00:10:53.360
do you tell your story about

00:10:54.821 --> 00:10:58.070
what you learned during your
analysis or the outcomes or the

00:10:58.070 --> 00:11:01.820
outputs from something? I'm sure
it's a trope by now,

00:11:01.820 --> 00:11:04.430
but like communication and being
able to tell a really

00:11:04.430 --> 00:11:06.530
nice story about what
it is that you did?

00:11:06.530 --> 00:11:10.010
What is it that would
improve, a decision maker's

00:11:11.180 --> 00:11:13.320
life is crucial.

00:11:13.320 --> 00:11:13.820
And I think

00:11:14.570 --> 00:11:17.810
one of the things that
data scientists or any analytics

00:11:17.810 --> 00:11:21.350
professional has to balance
is not only can you do the

00:11:21.350 --> 00:11:23.390
technical work, but can
you do the communication?

00:11:23.390 --> 00:11:25.640
And can you tell
that nice story arc?

00:11:26.580 --> 00:11:26.827
-

00:11:26.827 --> 00:11:29.940
What's one of those
communication or storytelling

00:11:29.940 --> 00:11:31.230
tips you'd share with us?

00:11:32.520 --> 00:11:33.540
- Less is more.

00:11:34.260 --> 00:11:36.570
I think, one of the--

00:11:36.570 --> 00:11:38.310
I think if you can have

00:11:39.210 --> 00:11:40.700
to go back to Posit Conf,

00:11:41.310 --> 00:11:43.657
for everyone's
awareness, if you do a

00:11:44.850 --> 00:11:48.180
talk at Posit Conf, you'll
go through like speaker training.

00:11:48.180 --> 00:11:52.320
And one of the first trainings
or maybe the second one or

00:11:52.320 --> 00:11:54.240
maybe it is just the first two,

00:11:54.240 --> 00:11:57.370
you focus on figuring out
what is your governing idea. Like,

00:11:57.370 --> 00:12:00.660
what is the one idea that
you want everyone to walk out from

00:12:00.660 --> 00:12:01.980
after seeing your talk?

00:12:01.980 --> 00:12:06.630
And I think that should be
applied to how you communicate

00:12:07.990 --> 00:12:09.600
with stakeholders.

00:12:09.600 --> 00:12:10.890
Less is more.

00:12:10.890 --> 00:12:14.370
Figure out what it is that
you want someone to walk away with

00:12:14.370 --> 00:12:15.840
based out of your
meeting. Because

00:12:16.820 --> 00:12:19.040
if you're trying to show
like a presentation,

00:12:19.780 --> 00:12:23.020
that presentation is not the
only thing that stakeholder is

00:12:23.020 --> 00:12:24.370
going to do in that day.

00:12:24.370 --> 00:12:27.535
They're going to be receiving
hundreds of emails per day.

00:12:28.660 --> 00:12:31.240
They're going to have to
be making decisions, things like

00:12:31.240 --> 00:12:35.060
that. So the question is like,
how do you make sure that they

00:12:35.800 --> 00:12:38.410
get that one piece of
crucial information you want them to

00:12:38.410 --> 00:12:40.210
have and really focus on that?

00:12:40.210 --> 00:12:43.870
So less is more. What is
your one governing idea?

00:12:43.870 --> 00:12:47.110
And then everything else
is just like extra gravy,

00:12:47.110 --> 00:12:47.830
if you can get it.

00:12:47.830 --> 00:12:52.180
But figure out what is that
one thing in order to make sure

00:12:52.180 --> 00:12:53.920
that people walk away with it.

00:12:54.550 --> 00:12:55.630
- Absolutely.

00:12:55.630 --> 00:12:58.900
I know a lot of people
have joined since the first

00:12:58.900 --> 00:13:01.390
beginning when I say how
people can jump into.

00:13:01.390 --> 00:13:05.065
So I'd like to just
sometimes recommend or remind people,

00:13:06.100 --> 00:13:09.280
if you have questions or things
you want to add to specific

00:13:09.280 --> 00:13:14.050
topics, feel free to jump in
by raising your hand here on Zoom.

00:13:14.050 --> 00:13:16.480
You can put questions
in the Zoom chat.

00:13:16.480 --> 00:13:19.490
And if it's something you want
me to read out loud instead,

00:13:19.490 --> 00:13:21.930
you can just put a
little star next to it.

00:13:21.930 --> 00:13:25.580
And then Curtis or Tyler will
reshare the Slido link in a

00:13:25.580 --> 00:13:29.570
second too, if you wanted
to ask anything anonymously as well.

00:13:30.450 --> 00:13:34.430
I realize sometimes I say that
too early when not everybody has joined.

00:13:35.990 --> 00:13:39.270
But Yanan, I see you asked
a question here in the chat.

00:13:39.270 --> 00:13:40.190
