00:15:02.230 --> 00:15:04.660
For those who are not
in the pharma industry,

00:15:04.660 --> 00:15:06.520
maybe I'll explain
what validation means,

00:15:06.520 --> 00:15:09.085
because it's like it's
an interesting problem.

00:15:10.660 --> 00:15:11.812
So within

00:15:13.420 --> 00:15:15.835
a biostats organization,
you basically have

00:15:16.960 --> 00:15:20.680
two things that you need
to accomplish to move assets or

00:15:20.680 --> 00:15:22.480
drugs through the pipeline.

00:15:22.480 --> 00:15:24.850
Basically, one,
can you tell what--

00:15:24.850 --> 00:15:28.940
can you trust the efficacy
of a drug? And you

00:15:29.740 --> 00:15:32.470
trust the safety of
a drug or an asset?

00:15:32.470 --> 00:15:37.030
And how you do that is you
write statistical code in order

00:15:37.030 --> 00:15:39.910
to be able to prove that, yes,

00:15:39.910 --> 00:15:43.150
we trust these statistical
tests that we've written.

00:15:45.100 --> 00:15:49.210
In order to do that, you
need to do something that's called validation

00:15:49.960 --> 00:15:52.570
for your software, which
is essentially to say,

00:15:53.260 --> 00:15:55.210
we have tested this software,

00:15:55.210 --> 00:15:59.770
and we believe that all the
outputs from this software is correct.

00:16:01.630 --> 00:16:05.090
How you do validation is
defined by the organization.

00:16:05.090 --> 00:16:08.875
But it's really important for you
to be able to trust your

00:16:10.180 --> 00:16:10.700
software.

00:16:10.700 --> 00:16:13.780
So there's a lot of testing
that goes into place in order

00:16:13.780 --> 00:16:17.990
to get that level of confidence
within an organization that

00:16:17.990 --> 00:16:20.390
says, yes, the software
can be trusted.

00:16:21.620 --> 00:16:24.810
And there's different nuances for
how you do the testing.

00:16:24.810 --> 00:16:26.580
So for example, if
you're using ggplot

00:16:28.070 --> 00:16:31.100
for creating outputs of
graphics, things like that,

00:16:31.100 --> 00:16:34.040
you can generally test
it through like code coverage,

00:16:34.040 --> 00:16:36.620
as well as and basically doing

00:16:37.220 --> 00:16:40.070
a couple like simple simulations.

00:16:41.030 --> 00:16:42.260
And saying like, OK,

00:16:42.260 --> 00:16:44.645
does it actually create the
output that we would expect,

00:16:46.960 --> 00:16:51.010
that we would expect if
you were to put it, basically

00:16:51.850 --> 00:16:52.940
create that output.

00:16:52.940 --> 00:16:55.270
So there's that but then
there's also output.

00:16:55.270 --> 00:16:57.700
There's validation from a
statistical correctness point

00:16:57.700 --> 00:17:00.430
of view. So for example,
if we're writing a

00:17:01.390 --> 00:17:05.770
piece of package to do
linear regression or doing a linear

00:17:05.770 --> 00:17:08.530
regression or using a package
for linear regression,

00:17:08.530 --> 00:17:11.020
how do you test
for that statistical correctness?

00:17:11.020 --> 00:17:14.470
And that's a little bit more
of a thornier problem to figure

00:17:14.470 --> 00:17:17.619
out, but it tends to
require a lot of different simulations,

00:17:17.619 --> 00:17:19.960
things like that, in order
to be able to say yes.

00:17:19.960 --> 00:17:22.180
From a statistical
correctness point of view,

00:17:22.180 --> 00:17:23.709
we trust this
piece of software.

