00:26:53.220 --> 00:26:56.550
working groups and different things
like that in order to be

00:26:56.550 --> 00:27:00.045
able to talk about R, as
well as be able to have

00:27:01.410 --> 00:27:04.545
industry standard for how
to use R for different ways

00:27:05.880 --> 00:27:06.930
for clinical trials.

00:27:06.930 --> 00:27:09.720
And we talk about things
that might sound silly,

00:27:09.720 --> 00:27:13.590
like how do you round in
R versus Sas and Python. But

00:27:13.590 --> 00:27:15.220
being able to talk very

00:27:15.960 --> 00:27:18.240
confidently about those
differences and why you might

00:27:18.240 --> 00:27:22.590
get differences in results is a
pretty crucial aspect for our industry.

00:27:23.670 --> 00:27:26.910
- I know you just mentioned the training piece.

00:27:27.510 --> 00:27:30.750
But I realize that's like
a huge undertaking with so many

00:27:30.750 --> 00:27:34.010
different trainees. And I was
wondering how do you scale

00:27:34.710 --> 00:27:36.390
that training out? I
think when we talked,

00:27:36.390 --> 00:27:38.250
you said that
there's 800 trainees?

00:27:39.330 --> 00:27:45.435
- Yeah. So within biostats,
GSK biostats, we're about 900 people.

00:27:46.090 --> 00:27:51.210
So what we started to
do initially was having just open

00:27:52.560 --> 00:27:55.995
in-person, as well as
virtual trainings with R.

00:27:56.520 --> 00:27:59.680
So like I think we got
to the point where 80%

00:28:00.360 --> 00:28:04.110
of biostats took an
introduction to Tidyverse R

00:28:04.110 --> 00:28:06.620
training, which is like 80%

00:28:07.500 --> 00:28:11.740
of 900 is like right
around 707, 720, something like that.

00:28:11.740 --> 00:28:16.410
And what we were finding
was people just weren't up taking

00:28:16.410 --> 00:28:19.080
the training and using
it on clinical trials.

00:28:19.080 --> 00:28:21.135
And the big reason we
found out was because

00:28:22.050 --> 00:28:25.050
you would take the training
and then you wouldn't actually use--

00:28:25.050 --> 00:28:25.550
wouldn't

00:28:27.000 --> 00:28:29.620
use R until like 12
or 18 months later.

00:28:29.620 --> 00:28:31.770
So what we ended up doing
was deciding like, all right,

00:28:31.770 --> 00:28:34.080
let's just put all R
training as on demand.

00:28:34.080 --> 00:28:36.780
Like I'm never going to write
a better like intro to Tidyverse

00:28:36.780 --> 00:28:41.370
training than either Hadley's written
or someone else has written online.

00:28:41.370 --> 00:28:44.220
Let's just make it all on
demand when you want to do it

00:28:44.220 --> 00:28:46.630
and focus purely on
support and mentoring.

00:28:46.630 --> 00:28:51.100
So what we do is we have
this small group of people,

00:28:51.100 --> 00:28:53.850
and they go, basically
study-to-study,

00:28:53.850 --> 00:28:56.890
whoever wants to use R
at that period of time,

00:28:56.890 --> 00:28:58.440
and we work with them.

00:28:58.440 --> 00:29:02.730
And the goal is basically train
that study team up and create

00:29:02.730 --> 00:29:05.940
them into champions and then
also give them the confidence

00:29:05.940 --> 00:29:08.400
to be able to go
to other studies and help provide

00:29:08.400 --> 00:29:10.270
guidance as well.

00:29:10.270 --> 00:29:12.630
So we do it
through that support and mentoring,

00:29:12.630 --> 00:29:15.390
but we also have
big community engagement through different

00:29:15.390 --> 00:29:18.070
like open forums where
people can ask questions,

00:29:18.070 --> 00:29:18.940
things like that.

00:29:18.940 --> 00:29:20.450
So it's really
focused on spending

00:29:21.210 --> 00:29:25.090
less time on creating
materials for training and documentation

00:29:25.090 --> 00:29:27.280
and more time on
figuring out, all right,

00:29:27.280 --> 00:29:30.220
how do we actually do support
and mentoring as well as get

00:29:30.220 --> 00:29:34.510
feedback to build tools that
actually will help our users

00:29:35.230 --> 00:29:38.035
for actually using R
on clinical trials?

00:29:38.710 --> 00:29:41.190
- And Ben, I see you have
a question in the chat,

00:29:41.190 --> 00:29:42.190
do you want to in here?

00:29:43.090 --> 00:29:43.590
- Yeah.

00:29:44.110 --> 00:29:46.480
I was curious, Ben, if you have

00:29:47.590 --> 00:29:51.950
other cross disciplines across GSK
as it's so large. I mean,

00:29:51.950 --> 00:29:53.125
obviously you have
a large enough

00:29:53.950 --> 00:29:55.930
group you're working with
to try and ramp them up.

00:29:55.930 --> 00:29:58.930
But it seems like there
could be other opportunities that would

00:29:59.740 --> 00:30:02.500
enhance the efficiency of the tools
and so on and so forth.

00:30:03.610 --> 00:30:05.920
- Yeah. There's lots of
different data science groups.

00:30:05.920 --> 00:30:09.700
Like I feel like sometimes,
you just stumble upon them, because

00:30:09.700 --> 00:30:11.515
I think GSK, I
think we're like

00:30:12.250 --> 00:30:15.715
100,000 people or something like
that, maybe a little bit less.

00:30:16.780 --> 00:30:18.070
And so you're just like,

00:30:19.520 --> 00:30:21.710
you're just like, you
just stumble upon them.

00:30:23.090 --> 00:30:25.670
For biostats, we're
pretty large luckily.

00:30:25.670 --> 00:30:28.460
So we work a lot with
our business partners.

00:30:29.360 --> 00:30:32.990
And the reason why we work
with them is like because biostats

00:30:32.990 --> 00:30:35.570
really focuses on moving
assets through the pipeline,

00:30:35.570 --> 00:30:38.600
we have direct impacts
on like HR or finance,

00:30:38.600 --> 00:30:40.640
so like we have
those connections that we do.

00:30:40.640 --> 00:30:42.185
But like we have a whole like

00:30:43.360 --> 00:30:46.660
AI group that's focused
on computational biology and

00:30:46.660 --> 00:30:48.890
computational chemistry and
different things like that.

00:30:48.890 --> 00:30:51.310
So there's lots of different--

00:30:52.660 --> 00:30:55.060
lots of different data
science groups within GSK.

00:30:56.230 --> 00:30:58.630
Some of them work across
departments but a lot of them

00:30:58.630 --> 00:31:01.060
tend to work like within
their own department,

00:31:01.060 --> 00:31:02.070
because they're so

00:31:02.770 --> 00:31:06.700
hyperfocused on specific problems
that they're trying to solve.

00:31:07.520 --> 00:31:07.530
-

00:31:07.530 --> 00:31:11.500
I was talking to someone at
the conference who works in pharma

00:31:11.500 --> 00:31:15.310
and hearing about some of
the different finance use cases

00:31:15.310 --> 00:31:19.510
within pharma, which I wouldn't
initially think about as well.

00:31:19.510 --> 00:31:20.170
- Yeah.

00:31:20.170 --> 00:31:20.260
-

00:31:20.260 --> 00:31:23.290
It's cool to hear about
all the different data science teams

00:31:23.290 --> 00:31:24.580
across companies.

00:31:27.010 --> 00:31:29.830
This was actually a Slido
question from your conference

00:31:29.830 --> 00:31:31.970
talk which I thought
because we didn't get to it,

00:31:31.970 --> 00:31:33.130
we could cover it here.

00:31:34.220 --> 00:31:35.320
But it was,

00:31:35.320 --> 00:31:39.010
how do you think about
redefining the way that people

00:31:39.010 --> 00:31:43.090
see themselves? Like helping
statisticians see themselves as

00:31:43.090 --> 00:31:45.595
programmers and using
Git for example.

00:31:47.550 --> 00:31:48.180
- Yeah.

00:31:48.180 --> 00:31:52.050
So I actually don't believe
in having people redefine themselves.

00:31:52.570 --> 00:31:54.400
I actually think about how

00:31:55.260 --> 00:31:56.580
do you help someone

00:31:57.300 --> 00:32:01.470
learn a tool or add a tool
to their toolkit in order to

00:32:01.470 --> 00:32:03.810
enhance the way that
they're working.

00:32:03.810 --> 00:32:05.205
Because at the end of the day,

00:32:06.470 --> 00:32:09.330
you are not going to turn
like a statistician into

00:32:10.270 --> 00:32:13.420
a programmer or a computer
scientist or things like that,

00:32:13.420 --> 00:32:14.965
unless they really
want to become one.

00:32:15.790 --> 00:32:19.270
So I think the goal is
like you don't actually try to redefine

00:32:19.270 --> 00:32:24.025
them. I think you try to
help them add tools to their toolkit

00:32:24.910 --> 00:32:26.510
in order to be able to--

00:32:28.900 --> 00:32:33.170
in order to be able to use
it in order to enhance their work.

00:32:33.170 --> 00:32:35.230
So like teaching is--

00:32:35.230 --> 00:32:37.615
there's teaching like
a statistician Git.

00:32:38.290 --> 00:32:41.410
Like you have to explain why
it's important for them to know,

00:32:42.370 --> 00:32:46.225
the hit by the bus
rule, if anyone's familiar with that.

00:32:47.530 --> 00:32:48.450
But yeah-- [INTERPOSING

00:32:48.450 --> 00:32:48.790
VOICES]

00:32:48.790 --> 00:32:51.460
- You use the, what if
you won the lottery--

00:32:51.460 --> 00:32:52.330
- Or yeah. What if--

00:32:52.330 --> 00:32:53.335
- You're hit by a bus.

00:32:53.860 --> 00:32:57.370
- Yeah. That's a better way
to put it, I guess. But yeah,

00:32:57.370 --> 00:33:00.430
so like I think it's for more
focus on how do you help people

00:33:00.430 --> 00:33:02.590
add and not
necessarily redefine.

00:33:03.130 --> 00:33:04.930
- Absolutely. And

00:33:05.620 --> 00:33:10.580
is this the training initiative
something that's come from the

00:33:10.580 --> 00:33:13.880
top at GSK? Or how was
it first rolled out?

00:33:14.660 --> 00:33:15.770
- Yeah. So

00:33:16.970 --> 00:33:18.590
I mean, it was more--

00:33:18.590 --> 00:33:19.610
it wasn't necessarily--

00:33:19.610 --> 00:33:21.330
it wasn't really rolled
out from the top.

00:33:21.330 --> 00:33:23.435
It was more like
we got the blessing

00:33:24.110 --> 00:33:26.220
from the leadership
team that says, hey,

00:33:26.220 --> 00:33:29.000
we want to try something
pretty different.

00:33:30.320 --> 00:33:34.940
Are you OK with this approach?
And got the thumbs up. And so

00:33:34.940 --> 00:33:36.965
we got the thumbs up to say,

00:33:38.810 --> 00:33:39.310
yeah,

00:33:39.680 --> 00:33:43.910
go and try this new way
to train, focus less on the

00:33:43.910 --> 00:33:45.950
classes, focus more on
support and mentoring.

00:33:45.950 --> 00:33:48.180
And that's how we
were able to do it.

00:33:48.180 --> 00:33:49.310
So it was--

00:33:49.310 --> 00:33:50.060
luckily,

00:33:50.060 --> 00:33:52.700
we were given the freedom and
the latitude to be able to try

00:33:52.700 --> 00:33:55.970
something new and in the
spirit of agile fail fast,

00:33:55.970 --> 00:33:59.675
if it didn't work. But it
has worked pretty well. So luckily,

00:34:00.440 --> 00:34:02.510
it wasn't something that
had to be top-down driven.

00:34:02.510 --> 00:34:06.110
And I think it's a model
that could be utilized pretty much

00:34:06.110 --> 00:34:07.670
in any organization.

00:34:08.770 --> 00:34:09.969
Because really,

00:34:09.969 --> 00:34:13.030
all you need is people who
are passionate about R and willing

00:34:13.030 --> 00:34:17.590
to spend some time to do
that support and training. So yeah.

00:34:18.130 --> 00:34:21.779
- I see a pretty big
conversation happening in the chat around

00:34:22.480 --> 00:34:22.980
rounding

00:34:23.590 --> 00:34:26.350
and can somebody explain to me,

00:34:26.350 --> 00:34:31.300
I've heard of this and how numbers
can be different in Sas versus R,

00:34:31.300 --> 00:34:34.465
but I would love to understand a
little bit more of what that means.

00:34:35.360 --> 00:34:37.180
I see a bunch of
links being shared.

00:34:37.900 --> 00:34:38.400
- Yeah.

00:34:39.010 --> 00:34:39.572
In general,

00:34:40.280 --> 00:34:43.210
so someone correct me if I
messed this up. But in general,

00:34:43.870 --> 00:34:47.800
computer science has adopted the
strategy that if you have

00:34:47.800 --> 00:34:50.189
something that ends
in 0.5, like 2.5,

00:34:51.489 --> 00:34:54.150
you around the
even numbers down.

00:34:55.210 --> 00:34:59.380
So like 2.5 becomes 2 and
the odd ones round up. So 3.5

00:34:59.380 --> 00:35:00.505
becomes 4.

00:35:01.630 --> 00:35:03.580
And so what that
does is on average,

00:35:03.580 --> 00:35:06.610
if you have a whole bunch
of numbers that end in 0.5,

00:35:06.610 --> 00:35:09.570
then the rounding up and
down like on average,

00:35:09.570 --> 00:35:11.030
doesn't really matter.

00:35:11.030 --> 00:35:12.150
SaaS on the other hand,

00:35:12.150 --> 00:35:14.930
is the only language that
will round everything in 0.5 up.

00:35:16.110 --> 00:35:16.850
So if you had a whole bunch,

00:35:16.850 --> 00:35:18.935
if you had a whole bunch
of numbers and you rounded them all,

00:35:19.610 --> 00:35:22.700
you would actually get an
average that is higher than the

00:35:22.700 --> 00:35:23.450
true average was.

00:35:23.450 --> 00:35:26.300
Because everything got rounded up
instead of like every other

00:35:26.300 --> 00:35:29.640
one getting rounded up. And
it's a very annoying nuance for

00:35:30.320 --> 00:35:34.520
submissions to FDA or
sponsors or investigators when they're

00:35:34.520 --> 00:35:35.735
used to seeing SaaS outputs

00:35:36.530 --> 00:35:38.510
and everything that's
0.5 being rounded up,

00:35:38.510 --> 00:35:39.590
they start to question that.

00:35:39.590 --> 00:35:42.020
And indeed at the FDA, if
you submit something in R,

00:35:42.980 --> 00:35:46.460
you might get a mismatch
in SaaS because of that rounding

00:35:46.460 --> 00:35:48.230
problem. It's a weird one.

00:35:48.230 --> 00:35:48.320
-

00:35:48.320 --> 00:35:51.020
Do you have to write
like custom functions to do R

00:35:51.020 --> 00:35:54.680
rounding like SaaS in order to
get around that or just explain

00:35:54.680 --> 00:35:56.870
it away via differences?

00:35:56.870 --> 00:35:58.130
- Yeah. Those do exist. I

00:35:58.130 --> 00:36:00.560
and Stephanie in here shared
a really good resource that

00:36:00.560 --> 00:36:03.560
looks like some packages
and other people have developed

00:36:03.560 --> 00:36:05.580
around this idea.
But yeah, for sure.

00:36:05.580 --> 00:36:06.080
I mean, you can.

00:36:06.080 --> 00:36:08.870
There are custom functions
that we sometimes write if they

00:36:08.870 --> 00:36:11.330
really just insist that
everything 0.5 rounds up, then

00:36:11.330 --> 00:36:12.305
we go, yeah. OK.

00:36:13.130 --> 00:36:14.510
And we just note it.

00:36:14.510 --> 00:36:16.820
- Thank you for
that explanation.

00:36:18.200 --> 00:36:19.220
Yeah, sometimes,

00:36:19.220 --> 00:36:21.710
it's like if something's
been said like enough times,

00:36:21.710 --> 00:36:24.470
it's like, do I pretend that
I know what that actually means?

00:36:24.470 --> 00:36:26.090
Or do we just stop and ask?

00:36:27.660 --> 00:36:30.210
Katherine, see you had a
question here in the chat.

00:36:30.210 --> 00:36:31.520
Do you want to jump in?

00:36:31.520 --> 00:36:32.020
- Yeah.

00:36:32.930 --> 00:36:35.990
You mentioned that you've
switched the way that you're

00:36:35.990 --> 00:36:39.800
training from a previous method
and that it's been successful.

00:36:39.800 --> 00:36:42.200
But how are you
measuring that success?

00:36:43.340 --> 00:36:46.070
We're currently rolling out
like a data science mentorship

00:36:46.070 --> 00:36:48.740
program and trying to figure
out how do we measure how

00:36:48.740 --> 00:36:51.680
successful we've been at
mentoring this person when we

00:36:51.680 --> 00:36:55.670
don't necessarily want to turn
them into a machine learning

00:36:55.670 --> 00:36:57.740
engineer or a
programmer or something.

00:36:57.740 --> 00:36:59.780
We just want them to be
better data scientists

00:37:00.470 --> 00:37:01.970
and data professionals.

00:37:02.590 --> 00:37:06.460
- Yeah. So for us
specifically, we're pretty--

00:37:07.720 --> 00:37:11.035
we try to keep it very simple,
which is basically like

00:37:11.710 --> 00:37:14.020
how many outputs are
we creating with R?

00:37:14.020 --> 00:37:16.840
And is that number increasing?
And if the answer is yes,

00:37:16.840 --> 00:37:19.390
then we're hitting our
metrics essentially.

00:37:19.390 --> 00:37:23.200
Or is the number of clinical trials
that we want to use R in

00:37:23.200 --> 00:37:24.550
some part of increasing?

00:37:24.550 --> 00:37:28.615
Then the answer is yes.
We believe that we are hitting

00:37:31.245 --> 00:37:34.270
our tracking or our metrics
in order to say yes,

00:37:34.270 --> 00:37:37.120
our enablement is increasing.

00:37:37.120 --> 00:37:39.820
If you're talking about
how do you make someone--

00:37:41.410 --> 00:37:42.280
how do you make--

00:37:42.280 --> 00:37:46.690
how do you track if someone
is improving their skills,

00:37:47.470 --> 00:37:49.650
to me, that's more
like a development plan

00:37:51.040 --> 00:37:53.080
point of view at an
individual level.

00:37:54.600 --> 00:37:59.880
But I think it's really hard. I think
you can only do it in terms of--

00:37:59.880 --> 00:38:01.260
if you really want to track,

00:38:01.260 --> 00:38:04.550
I think it has to be like
at an aggregate output level

00:38:05.610 --> 00:38:09.150
and not necessarily, did
this person like comprehend,

00:38:09.150 --> 00:38:10.820
unless it's like
at a development

00:38:11.700 --> 00:38:14.980
or a career or personal
development level, if that makes sense.

00:38:14.980 --> 00:38:16.660
- Yeah, I think that
makes a lot of sense,

00:38:16.660 --> 00:38:19.140
especially given that your
team's goal is R enablement.

00:38:19.140 --> 00:38:22.480
That's something that's fairly
straightforward to track.

00:38:22.480 --> 00:38:25.620
Are we seeing higher
use of R? Yeah.

00:38:25.620 --> 00:38:28.350
We're doing the professional
development part,

00:38:28.350 --> 00:38:31.860
was a lot harder. We
had a couple ideas. But really,

00:38:31.860 --> 00:38:34.920
one of those things is,
are they using Git now?

00:38:34.920 --> 00:38:38.145
And that's something that we're
tracking as part of that too.

00:38:39.460 --> 00:38:41.425
- Yeah. That gets really hard,

00:38:42.040 --> 00:38:44.920
having gone through like those
wars with like trying to teach

00:38:44.920 --> 00:38:45.880
people how to use it,

00:38:45.880 --> 00:38:50.080
like it's the steepest learning
curve that I think I've ever

00:38:50.080 --> 00:38:53.370
encountered with teams. It just--

00:38:53.370 --> 00:38:54.730
it's really, really tough.

00:38:55.390 --> 00:38:58.030
And there's just so
many points of friction.

00:38:58.030 --> 00:39:00.700
Just like when you get
your first merge conflict and you

00:39:00.700 --> 00:39:02.030
just don't know what to do.

00:39:02.030 --> 00:39:04.750
And it seems like the world
just like will not let you

00:39:04.750 --> 00:39:08.750
merge your branches together.
Like it is a really,

00:39:08.750 --> 00:39:09.590
really tough thing.

00:39:10.420 --> 00:39:13.345
- You just unprotect your branches
and merge them anyway. It's fine.

00:39:14.390 --> 00:39:14.830
- Yeah.

00:39:14.830 --> 00:39:16.990
But then you got to explain
it to someone later on

00:39:16.990 --> 00:39:18.220
reproducibility, right?

00:39:19.780 --> 00:39:20.380
- I see

00:39:21.160 --> 00:39:23.540
Rebecca had a question. There's
a star, so I'll read it.

00:39:23.540 --> 00:39:25.090
But it expands on this.

00:39:25.090 --> 00:39:28.240
But how are you like
demonstrating or showing that

00:39:28.240 --> 00:39:29.515
evidence for it working?

00:39:30.400 --> 00:39:31.520
Like are you--

00:39:31.520 --> 00:39:32.530
I guess, to expand on this,

00:39:32.530 --> 00:39:35.785
are you presenting this
to anybody as well?

00:39:38.620 --> 00:39:40.600
- What specifically
in terms of what?

00:39:40.600 --> 00:39:41.365
- I think about

00:39:42.220 --> 00:39:45.100
to expand on how
you're measuring or tracking the

00:39:45.100 --> 00:39:46.900
success against the
previous method.

00:39:48.160 --> 00:39:51.220
- Yeah. I mean, I
don't think we--

00:39:51.220 --> 00:39:51.490
I mean,

00:39:51.490 --> 00:39:55.510
we're not trying to get to
a world where R replaces SaaS.

00:39:55.510 --> 00:39:58.300
We want to live in a world
where you can select the tool

00:39:58.300 --> 00:40:01.395
that you want to use to
create your outputs. So

00:40:02.110 --> 00:40:05.090
if our goal is to
make R a primary

00:40:05.830 --> 00:40:07.070
programming language

00:40:09.070 --> 00:40:11.380
or a tool that can be
used, we should be--

00:40:11.380 --> 00:40:14.680
and there's an interest, then we
should be able to see increase

00:40:15.400 --> 00:40:16.390
of the use of R.

00:40:16.390 --> 00:40:17.912
So like don't look at it as--

00:40:19.690 --> 00:40:21.610
I look at it as like
you have options,

00:40:21.610 --> 00:40:24.160
and we want to make
sure those options are being utilized.

00:40:25.030 --> 00:40:30.130
And not like how do I get
R usage from 10% to 100%

00:40:30.130 --> 00:40:31.780
within my organization.

00:40:31.780 --> 00:40:32.560
- Thank you.

00:40:33.660 --> 00:40:36.100
Kevin, I see you had
a question around the cultural barriers.

00:40:36.100 --> 00:40:37.240
Want to jump in?

00:40:37.240 --> 00:40:40.360
- Yeah. Sure. Yeah. I think
you spoke a little bit to this,

00:40:40.360 --> 00:40:43.570
but I was just curious if
you could elaborate more on how you

00:40:43.570 --> 00:40:48.050
dealt with cultural barriers to
adopting open source in pharma,

00:40:48.050 --> 00:40:50.000
especially like, yeah,
it's a regulated industry.

00:40:50.000 --> 00:40:52.730
And almost want
to frame it like,

00:40:52.730 --> 00:40:56.870
was it more helpful to show
the efficacy of open source?

00:40:56.870 --> 00:40:59.330
Or was it more important
to show the safety of it?

00:40:59.330 --> 00:41:02.600
Like or this both important?
You know what I mean?

00:41:02.600 --> 00:41:07.490
- Yeah. Yeah. So the big
thing that actually was the major--

00:41:10.410 --> 00:41:13.350
major way that we broke a lot
of ground in terms of open

00:41:13.350 --> 00:41:15.270
source was visualizations.

00:41:15.270 --> 00:41:19.140
So because one of the big
things that R does so well is

00:41:20.100 --> 00:41:23.300
ggplot and being able to
create awesome visualizations

00:41:24.480 --> 00:41:27.360
with data. And that
was something that SaaS

00:41:28.320 --> 00:41:29.890
really, really struggled with.

00:41:29.890 --> 00:41:33.150
And so the wedge that we
actually figured out in order

00:41:33.150 --> 00:41:36.640
to be able to start having
the open source conversation was like,

00:41:36.640 --> 00:41:39.720
look at all these cool
like visualizations and look how

00:41:39.720 --> 00:41:41.670
easy it is to make
these visualizations

00:41:42.750 --> 00:41:43.810
compared to other

00:41:44.610 --> 00:41:46.200
programming languages.

00:41:46.200 --> 00:41:51.282
Why don't we like start looking at open
source as a way to be able to

00:41:54.060 --> 00:41:56.730
basically use tools
successfully? And then

00:41:57.480 --> 00:41:58.660
a lot of things happened.

00:41:58.660 --> 00:42:01.020
But I think the big
another thing that really has broken

00:42:01.020 --> 00:42:04.540
through cultural barriers
has been there's a really,

00:42:04.540 --> 00:42:06.020
really strong industry,

00:42:06.840 --> 00:42:08.370
call it collaboration,

00:42:08.370 --> 00:42:10.840
called like the
Pharmaverse where different

00:42:11.620 --> 00:42:14.060
companies, so like Roche, GSK,

00:42:15.190 --> 00:42:16.600
Novartis, J&amp;amp;J,

00:42:16.600 --> 00:42:19.090
like all these different
companies are creating open

00:42:19.090 --> 00:42:23.230
source packages for the
clinical trial development

00:42:23.230 --> 00:42:25.480
pipeline. So like
if you can set,

00:42:25.480 --> 00:42:28.360
if you as an industry can
agree upon like we all trust these

00:42:28.360 --> 00:42:29.320
open source tools,

00:42:29.980 --> 00:42:32.800
it really kind of helps
to break down those cultural

00:42:32.800 --> 00:42:35.920
barriers about why should I
use this open source tool.

00:42:35.920 --> 00:42:38.480
And it's really easy
to say like, well,

00:42:38.480 --> 00:42:42.010
we want to use it because
Roche is using it or we've developed

00:42:42.010 --> 00:42:44.320
it with Roche and with--

00:42:44.320 --> 00:42:44.820
insert

00:42:45.370 --> 00:42:48.410
other pharma company.
And you can say, we

00:42:49.150 --> 00:42:51.320
helped create it,
we should trust it.

00:42:51.320 --> 00:42:54.190
And so I think that has
been one of the things that has

00:42:54.190 --> 00:42:55.480
helped with those
cultural barriers.

00:42:57.110 --> 00:42:58.340
- Did you just get--

00:42:58.340 --> 00:42:59.120
one follow up,

00:42:59.120 --> 00:43:01.860
did you ever hit a wall where
there's some parts of the

00:43:01.860 --> 00:43:03.500
organization or some people,

00:43:03.500 --> 00:43:06.770
some managers who just
like can't get over

00:43:08.240 --> 00:43:12.530
the validation or the concerns
about open source? Or did you

00:43:12.530 --> 00:43:16.640
find you were able to pretty much
break through in most when you needed to?

00:43:16.640 --> 00:43:18.530
- I think you can break
through. But at the end of the

00:43:18.530 --> 00:43:21.990
day, like I try to
talk a little bit about earlier,

00:43:21.990 --> 00:43:24.050
like if people
want to use their

00:43:24.830 --> 00:43:26.780
proprietary tool, fine.

00:43:26.780 --> 00:43:29.330
If it helps them do their work
like at the end of the day like

00:43:29.330 --> 00:43:31.550
all I care about is like,
can you do your work?

00:43:31.550 --> 00:43:34.130
And can you do your work in
a timely manner and not have

00:43:34.130 --> 00:43:37.130
bottlenecks with platforms?
And if the answer is yes,

00:43:37.130 --> 00:43:40.190
then who cares? If that's
your preferred tool,

00:43:40.190 --> 00:43:41.570
that's where you like
to spend your time,

00:43:41.570 --> 00:43:43.700
then I'm not going to
tell you differently.

00:43:44.270 --> 00:43:45.170
- That makes
sense. Thanks, man.

00:43:45.950 --> 00:43:48.687
- Thank you all for
the great questions. If

00:43:50.150 --> 00:43:52.790
anybody feels like their
questions are getting missed,

00:43:52.790 --> 00:43:54.410
I'm sorry. If we missed
them in the chat,

00:43:54.410 --> 00:43:58.250
so feel free to repost below
in the Zoom chat as well.

00:44:00.170 --> 00:44:02.850
But there was one
anonymous question

00:44:03.530 --> 00:44:04.590
that was--

00:44:06.030 --> 00:44:06.530
when

00:44:07.350 --> 00:44:11.970
we say like what the FDA
wants to see, what does that

00:44:11.970 --> 00:44:15.780
actually mean? Like do those
statements come directly from FDA

00:44:16.380 --> 00:44:19.200
employees? Or where
do people hear that?

00:44:19.200 --> 00:44:20.310
- In terms of--

00:44:20.310 --> 00:44:23.580
I mean, you could talk a little
bit about the FDA in terms of--

00:44:23.580 --> 00:44:24.080
I

00:44:24.720 --> 00:44:26.700
think there's like
guidance on software.

00:44:26.700 --> 00:44:27.930
Like if I was Phil Bowsher,

00:44:27.930 --> 00:44:32.560
I would have the PDF up already
on what it is that you can use.

00:44:32.560 --> 00:44:33.990
But I think there's a lot of--

00:44:33.990 --> 00:44:34.490
when

00:44:35.190 --> 00:44:38.970
you submit something to an
FDA or another regulatory agency,

00:44:38.970 --> 00:44:42.130
it's like an open conversation.
And you have a conversation.

00:44:42.130 --> 00:44:43.080
And so it's not just like--

00:44:43.080 --> 00:44:45.720
it's not a piece of homework
where I have to submit this at

00:44:45.720 --> 00:44:47.790
midnight and then
I get a grade.

00:44:47.790 --> 00:44:50.250
There's like an ongoing
back and forth conversation that

00:44:50.250 --> 00:44:51.820
occurs. And so--

00:44:54.700 --> 00:44:57.960
being able to engage and
have those conversations is where

00:44:57.960 --> 00:45:02.460
you learn exactly what it
that is needed from a regulator.

00:45:02.460 --> 00:45:05.770
Now there's expected standards and
you know what standards it

00:45:05.770 --> 00:45:09.280
is that you have to hit
in terms of having a successful

00:45:09.280 --> 00:45:10.480
submission or

00:45:11.380 --> 00:45:15.220
ad hoc analysis or whatever it
might to be able to answer a

00:45:15.220 --> 00:45:18.070
scientific question.
But it's not--

00:45:18.070 --> 00:45:21.170
like I said, it's not it's
an ongoing conversation.

00:45:21.170 --> 00:45:24.715
It's not like a submit your homework
and you hope you get a good grade.

00:45:25.780 --> 00:45:26.590
- Thank you, Ben.

00:45:28.300 --> 00:45:31.550
Ahmed, I see you had a question
you just put into the chat.

00:45:31.550 --> 00:45:32.545
Do you want to jump in here?

00:45:33.340 --> 00:45:33.895
- Yeah, sure.

00:45:34.450 --> 00:45:36.535
For the different Bayesian

00:45:38.680 --> 00:45:40.060
statistics that could be used,

00:45:40.960 --> 00:45:45.100
I gather it's going to be
different between discovery and

00:45:45.100 --> 00:45:48.850
like drug discovery or clinical
trial data analysis versus

00:45:48.850 --> 00:45:51.170
sales or things of that nature.

00:45:51.170 --> 00:45:53.260
And I'm not sure if
Ben deals with both.

00:45:53.890 --> 00:45:55.180
But I was curious if

00:45:56.080 --> 00:46:00.100
some of the algorithms that
are proprietary could or have been

00:46:01.720 --> 00:46:04.015
used in R or developed in R.

00:46:05.020 --> 00:46:07.080
- I can't speak specific about

00:46:08.530 --> 00:46:09.220
Bayesian lab.

00:46:10.000 --> 00:46:14.920
I can tell you our approach
to Bayesian statistics is

00:46:14.920 --> 00:46:17.780
open source. It's published
in different journal articles,

00:46:17.780 --> 00:46:20.890
things like that. We
focus on Bayesian dynamic borrowing.

00:46:20.890 --> 00:46:21.980
That's like our

00:46:22.840 --> 00:46:25.600
focus in terms of how it
is that we use Bayesian stats.

00:46:25.600 --> 00:46:26.500
But I can't--

00:46:26.500 --> 00:46:28.990
I don't know enough about some
of the stuff that goes on in

00:46:28.990 --> 00:46:30.865
Bayesian lab to be able
to answer that question.

