00:46:41.000 --> 00:46:43.310
I'll stop calling on somebody
if they talk too much.

00:46:43.310 --> 00:46:43.670
[LAUGHS]

00:46:43.670 --> 00:46:44.270
- OK. Good.

00:46:44.990 --> 00:46:45.490
Ben,

00:46:46.010 --> 00:46:49.340
I'm sure, like all of us in
the pharma adjacent space,

00:46:49.340 --> 00:46:51.020
you probably watched
the Novo Nordisk talk,

00:46:51.020 --> 00:46:53.390
the Journey to NR Submission.
Yeah, you're nodding. Yeah. Yeah.

00:46:53.390 --> 00:46:55.370
It was awesome. We were
all waiting for that one.

00:46:55.370 --> 00:46:56.360
And they're very open for--

00:46:56.360 --> 00:46:57.470
everyone who didn't see it,

00:46:57.470 --> 00:47:00.860
about challenges with
submitting things that the FDA

00:47:00.860 --> 00:47:01.850
maybe hasn't seen before.

00:47:02.460 --> 00:47:04.430
They're like they
submitted several times.

00:47:04.430 --> 00:47:06.260
You have to do is like
nah, this doesn't work on my machine.

00:47:06.260 --> 00:47:09.440
And then to Ben's point
about it being a conversation, they

00:47:10.040 --> 00:47:11.520
had a conversation, and
they work through it.

00:47:11.520 --> 00:47:12.807
So I was wondering
if you had any just

00:47:14.000 --> 00:47:16.610
gut reactions about that
talk, like any further commentary,

00:47:16.610 --> 00:47:18.545
you might color, you might want
to add to what they showed?

00:47:20.690 --> 00:47:21.190
- Yeah.

00:47:23.820 --> 00:47:26.265
There are two things that I
really took away from that talk.

00:47:27.240 --> 00:47:27.740
One,

00:47:28.380 --> 00:47:30.230
it was a really similar story.

00:47:31.050 --> 00:47:33.810
We all are solving the same
problem albeit in our different

00:47:33.810 --> 00:47:36.990
ways based off like our
organizational culture or just

00:47:36.990 --> 00:47:38.850
organizational preferences.

00:47:39.930 --> 00:47:40.430
The

00:47:41.130 --> 00:47:43.515
big thing I took away
from it actually is

00:47:44.650 --> 00:47:49.150
if we are going down the
open source route in terms of

00:47:49.150 --> 00:47:52.180
R and different R
packages, things like that,

00:47:52.720 --> 00:47:55.780
I think it's actually going
to potentially create a stronger

00:47:55.780 --> 00:47:58.970
culture of open source tools.

00:47:59.830 --> 00:48:00.330
Because

00:48:01.240 --> 00:48:02.420
it seemed like the--

00:48:03.910 --> 00:48:04.990
and by that, I mean,

00:48:05.830 --> 00:48:06.840
I don't know if

00:48:07.600 --> 00:48:10.230
specific companies will
be able to have internal

00:48:10.930 --> 00:48:14.935
tools that only they can use
because of the issues that Novo had

00:48:15.970 --> 00:48:18.730
with using their own internal
tools and trying to do a

00:48:18.730 --> 00:48:22.660
submission because of how do I
get them to be able to install

00:48:22.660 --> 00:48:25.210
this package, which might
not be available on CRAN,

00:48:25.210 --> 00:48:27.790
how do I be able to do this?
So I think it actually--

00:48:27.790 --> 00:48:30.490
one of the things that it's
made me think a lot about is

00:48:30.490 --> 00:48:31.750
how much

00:48:32.740 --> 00:48:36.910
internally do we actually
want to create because of the

00:48:36.910 --> 00:48:40.810
hiccups that could cause in
the actual submission process?

00:48:40.810 --> 00:48:44.480
And will that potentially
result in a stronger

00:48:45.550 --> 00:48:50.920
open source ecosystem? Because of
just like, just the making

00:48:50.920 --> 00:48:55.420
it easier in order for
regulators, whether it be FDA,

00:48:55.420 --> 00:48:58.795
EMA, Japan, whatever it might to
be able to use these tools.

00:48:59.710 --> 00:49:01.610
- That's such a great answer.
Yeah, I felt the same.

00:49:01.610 --> 00:49:04.130
There was that one part
in their talk where they said,

00:49:04.130 --> 00:49:05.740
well the solution was just
we needed to open source

00:49:05.740 --> 00:49:08.620
everything, and then the FDA was
happy. And I was like, oh,

00:49:08.620 --> 00:49:09.910
I didn't expect that.

00:49:09.910 --> 00:49:11.320
But it's almost like--

00:49:11.320 --> 00:49:14.410
it's a good advisory tip
for those of us developing internal

00:49:14.410 --> 00:49:18.280
packages at our companies, like
that may not be a sustainable

00:49:18.280 --> 00:49:20.980
journey in the future
when we go to submit.

00:49:20.980 --> 00:49:21.460
- Yeah.

00:49:21.460 --> 00:49:23.980
And I think the big thing that
I have been thinking a lot

00:49:23.980 --> 00:49:26.380
about is the reason why
we've never encountered this,

00:49:26.380 --> 00:49:29.440
we being the pharma industry has
never encountered this, is because say,

00:49:30.220 --> 00:49:32.840
SaaS is your tool of
choice. Theoretically,

00:49:32.840 --> 00:49:35.720
SaaS is the same from
one organization to

00:49:36.440 --> 00:49:38.900
the other. And I keep harping
on this, so I'm sorry,

00:49:38.900 --> 00:49:41.340
but like
reproducibility is key.

00:49:41.340 --> 00:49:43.100
So if it exists in tool--

00:49:43.100 --> 00:49:45.860
if your proprietary tool exists
in two organizations in the

00:49:45.860 --> 00:49:47.945
same version XYZ,

00:49:48.980 --> 00:49:52.760
then you have to make sure that
that's guaranteed if you're

00:49:52.760 --> 00:49:53.990
using open source tools.

00:49:53.990 --> 00:49:54.668
And from

00:49:56.660 --> 00:49:57.770
a philosophical point of view,

00:49:57.770 --> 00:50:00.410
that probably means we have to
open source more to make sure

00:50:00.410 --> 00:50:03.700
that everyone has access,
and we don't have these very

00:50:04.700 --> 00:50:08.720
secret internal tools that
cause issues with submissions.

00:50:08.720 --> 00:50:09.275
- Thank you.

00:50:10.730 --> 00:50:11.630
Ben, a question

00:50:12.380 --> 00:50:14.840
I'd love to get to
ask people is about

00:50:15.590 --> 00:50:19.040
career advice they've received over
the course of their career

00:50:19.040 --> 00:50:21.815
or have given. Do you
have something that stands out?

00:50:22.580 --> 00:50:23.120
- Oh, man.

00:50:24.920 --> 00:50:28.955
I think the thing that always stuck
with me is it's OK to say no.

00:50:30.140 --> 00:50:33.290
Like you should be ruthlessly
prioritizing your time and

00:50:33.290 --> 00:50:35.550
trying to work on things
you're passionate about.

00:50:35.550 --> 00:50:38.690
So it's OK to say no to
certain things if you

00:50:39.470 --> 00:50:40.700
are the wrong fit.

00:50:41.490 --> 00:50:43.850
It's also, if you think
about your brand management,

00:50:43.850 --> 00:50:45.920
it's sometimes
nice to be wanted.

00:50:45.920 --> 00:50:50.180
So saying no sometimes is good
for that brand management.

00:50:50.180 --> 00:50:54.220
So I think being comfortable
with the word no and

00:50:54.980 --> 00:50:56.370
is really important.

00:50:56.370 --> 00:51:00.500
The other thing and I think
is really important or the other

00:51:00.500 --> 00:51:01.880
piece of advice I
was given to me,

00:51:01.880 --> 00:51:05.450
and I try to live by
this value is like be radically transparent.

00:51:06.050 --> 00:51:07.175
So meaning,

00:51:08.500 --> 00:51:12.690
no matter what, just be like,
just no such thing as holding

00:51:13.390 --> 00:51:16.480
cards close to your chest
basically say like here's what

00:51:16.480 --> 00:51:17.860
I got. What do you have?

00:51:18.460 --> 00:51:22.120
And really focus on being able
to have those open and honest

00:51:22.120 --> 00:51:25.550
conversations, which I think
leads to greater vulnerability,

00:51:25.550 --> 00:51:27.400
which is like how you make
connections with people

00:51:28.750 --> 00:51:31.630
and really are able to
be empathetic with people and

00:51:31.630 --> 00:51:33.260
trying to understand
their problems.

00:51:33.260 --> 00:51:36.520
So I think being able to say no

00:51:37.150 --> 00:51:41.170
and also being able to
be radically transparent is really crucial

00:51:41.890 --> 00:51:43.360
work advice that people,

00:51:43.360 --> 00:51:45.850
that a lot of mentors
have given me in the past.

00:51:46.570 --> 00:51:49.885
- Absolutely. Thank you
so much for sharing that.

