what are people facing?

00:52:37.830 --> 00:52:38.330
And

00:52:39.120 --> 00:52:42.420
one of the things that I've
learned is like every--

00:52:45.030 --> 00:52:47.430
or one thing, one learning
I've had from it is like,

00:52:47.430 --> 00:52:49.065
no one's trying
to do a bad job.

00:52:50.050 --> 00:52:52.170
It's just there's so many
things that are going on in

00:52:52.170 --> 00:52:53.430
life that you have to--

00:52:54.360 --> 00:52:57.270
people require like certain
different levels of support.

00:52:57.270 --> 00:52:58.590
They all want to try--

00:52:58.590 --> 00:53:01.020
they all want to do the
new cool thing. It's just--

00:53:01.020 --> 00:53:02.890
everyone's at different stages.

00:53:02.890 --> 00:53:05.490
So I think being able to
connect with different teams,

00:53:05.490 --> 00:53:07.050
understand how it is--

00:53:08.754 --> 00:53:10.920
where they are in their point
of life or where they are in

00:53:10.920 --> 00:53:13.740
their like development journey in order
to be able to do these things,

00:53:14.580 --> 00:53:17.250
it's really important to
be empathetic and learn

00:53:17.850 --> 00:53:20.950
and be able to take those
learnings and bring it to other teams.

00:53:20.950 --> 00:53:24.390
And I think that's one thing
that I find really cool about

00:53:24.390 --> 00:53:26.940
my job is like being
able to connect with these different

00:53:26.940 --> 00:53:29.650
teams, bringing learnings
from different places

00:53:30.690 --> 00:53:33.210
and bringing it to other
teams, make those connections.

00:53:33.210 --> 00:53:37.250
As well as bring what are the
problems that people are facing to our

00:53:38.070 --> 00:53:41.100
different tools teams or to my
tools team to be able to say

00:53:41.100 --> 00:53:42.825
like, here are things
that I'm hearing.

00:53:44.890 --> 00:53:46.480
What are your thoughts?
And I think like,

00:53:47.020 --> 00:53:48.610
a great example of this was

00:53:50.170 --> 00:53:52.300
another talk at
Posit Conf from GSK,

00:53:52.300 --> 00:53:54.850
we created this open
source tool called Slushy,

00:53:54.850 --> 00:53:59.770
which is built on top of RM
do environment management.

00:53:59.770 --> 00:54:00.270
So

00:54:00.940 --> 00:54:03.400
I don't know if we would have
ever gotten to that point

00:54:03.400 --> 00:54:05.920
without being able to go out
to these different teams and like

00:54:05.920 --> 00:54:08.710
actually listen to what
are their problems for

00:54:09.610 --> 00:54:11.852
what they're encountering
if they want to use R

00:54:13.000 --> 00:54:14.380
for that creation
of their outputs.

00:54:14.980 --> 00:54:15.640
- Yeah.

00:54:15.640 --> 00:54:18.760
Was trying to go find the
link to that talk but I forgot

00:54:18.760 --> 00:54:22.315
they're not all public yet but they
will be in the next few months.

00:54:23.770 --> 00:54:28.060
Ben, was there anything
that after your talk,

00:54:28.060 --> 00:54:28.930
you thought about, oh,

00:54:28.930 --> 00:54:32.580
I wish I also covered this
or maybe a question that was asked

00:54:33.280 --> 00:54:35.830
of you that you had
wanted to expand on.

00:54:38.470 --> 00:54:40.285
- I think the
biggest thing is--

00:54:42.110 --> 00:54:44.130
if you really want to--

00:54:46.050 --> 00:54:48.030
if you really want
to enable learning,

00:54:48.030 --> 00:54:51.040
you actually need to put
the support behind it.

00:54:51.040 --> 00:54:55.410
And I think the big thing that
I wish I was more focused on

00:54:55.410 --> 00:54:56.910
in that talk was,

00:54:57.880 --> 00:55:00.460
can do as much
training as you want.

00:55:01.610 --> 00:55:03.235
But if you're not
actually using it

00:55:04.260 --> 00:55:05.910
in the creation of an output,

00:55:05.910 --> 00:55:09.460
that's where you actually
learn how to do things.

00:55:09.460 --> 00:55:13.080
And there needs to be a
really robust support team behind to

00:55:13.080 --> 00:55:14.470
help answer those questions.

00:55:14.470 --> 00:55:18.630
And I wish I hit that
more is just really focused on that

00:55:18.630 --> 00:55:19.710
support and mentoring.

00:55:19.710 --> 00:55:22.000
It's really hard to scale,
I realize, because it's actual

00:55:22.740 --> 00:55:26.280
hours and not a piece of
document that someone can go

00:55:26.280 --> 00:55:29.850
read but that support and
mentorship is crucial for being

00:55:29.850 --> 00:55:33.255
able to change and enable
an organization to use a tool.

00:55:34.280 --> 00:55:34.430
-

00:55:34.430 --> 00:55:36.830
And I'm sorry if I missed
this in the beginning when you were

00:55:36.830 --> 00:55:39.845
describing the different roles in
the team, but is there this

00:55:40.610 --> 00:55:43.640
idea of a help desk or
center of excellence or would people

00:55:43.640 --> 00:55:46.190
go for additional support?

00:55:46.190 --> 00:55:48.690
- Yeah. So that's what
that Accelerate R team is.

00:55:48.690 --> 00:55:51.650
And we have a giant community
forum where people are able to

00:55:51.650 --> 00:55:53.315
post questions as well.

00:55:54.070 --> 00:55:57.380
I think we call it like
R for clinical forum and people will

00:55:57.380 --> 00:56:00.320
just post questions or post
announcements about new

00:56:00.320 --> 00:56:02.100
packages and things like that.

00:56:02.100 --> 00:56:04.860
So we have not only
a the direct support,

00:56:04.860 --> 00:56:08.690
but we also have the a robust
community that has a Teams

00:56:08.690 --> 00:56:10.085
channel where there's an active

00:56:11.720 --> 00:56:14.480
communication that occurs that
answer questions or celebrate

00:56:14.480 --> 00:56:15.830
wins things like that.

